From 68dd7c97ae304195ffbed3b7cb6a3b6fb56eea8d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: patricksmithlaravel <patricksmithlaravel@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 23:30:28 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ios: implement Window::set_ime_purpose

Window::set_ime_purpose only logged that it is ignored on iOS, so UIKit
took the default text input traits for every field: a password field was
not secure text entry, and nothing asked the keyboard to leave
autocorrection, auto-capitalization or smart punctuation out of a
password or a terminal.

The view now keeps the purpose and answers the optional
UITextInputTraits getters that UIKit reads from the first responder to
set up the keyboard. ImePurpose::Password answers isSecureTextEntry
YES, autocapitalizationType None, and autocorrectionType,
spellCheckingType, smartQuotesType, smartDashesType and
smartInsertDeleteType No. ImePurpose::Terminal answers the same except
isSecureTextEntry NO. ImePurpose::Normal answers the defaults that
UITextInputTraits.h documents (autocapitalizationType Sentences,
isSecureTextEntry NO, the others Default), which UIKit also takes for a
getter the responder does not implement, so a normal field's keyboard
does not change.

UIKit reads the traits when the view becomes the first responder, and
an app may set the purpose after that, for example right after it
allows the IME. So when the purpose changes while the view is the first
responder, the view reloads its input views for the keyboard to pick
the new traits up. Setting the purpose it already has does nothing, so
an app that sets it again whenever the cursor area changes does not
reload the keyboard each time.

To be proposed upstream; there is no pull request yet.

Verified: rustfmt +nightly --check with winit's rustfmt.toml on the
changed files; cargo clippy --lib --tests -D warnings, cargo doc
-D warnings and cargo test --lib --no-run for aarch64-apple-ios-sim.
With UIKit under Mac Catalyst (macOS 27 SDK), -[UITextInputTraits
takeTraitsFrom:] took byte-identical traits from a UIKeyInput object
that answers the defaults above and from one that implements none of
the getters, and the answers for Password and Terminal as listed. Not
yet seen on a simulator or device: the view adopts only UIKeyInput, and
its keyboard on the simulator shows no predictions and capitalizes
nothing in any field, with or without this change, so what the traits
change for it there has not been observed.
